[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
No network-side outage is present. From the complaining workstation (host_1_1_1_1), both affected sites resolve correctly and are fully reachable over HTTP. Evidence: DNS lookups return web2.local → 10.200.0.5 and web3.local → 10.200.0.6 (getent hosts). ICMP from host_1_1_1_1 to both servers succeeds with low latency and 0% loss. HTTP checks from the same host to http://web2.local and http://web3.local complete in ~4 ms and return HTTP 200 with the expected “Welcome to Web Server 2/3” content. On the servers themselves, netstat shows nginx/httpd listening on 0.0.0.0:80 on both web_server_2 and web_server_3. Other devices on the server VLAN (e.g., dns_server) can also reach them with no loss. This rules out a shared link or switch_server_access failure—the switch is forwarding normally for all attached servers.

Mechanism: If the access switch had failed, ICMP/HTTP to both servers would fail broadly from multiple sources; instead, end-to-end L3/L7 paths are healthy. Localization: Not in the network core, distribution, access switch, or servers; behavior is not reproducible from host_1_1_1_1 during testing. Root cause: No network fault observed; the earlier symptom was likely transient or client-side (e.g., browser/DNS cache) and not due to the shared switch.

Corrective fix: No network changes required. Advise clearing browser and DNS cache on host_1_1_1_1 and retry; if it recurs, capture timestamp/URL and provide a packet trace for further client-side analysis.
=== END ===